# {{.Name}}

Run `graft dev`, then open http://localhost:8080 and http://localhost:8080/swagger.
Use `graft build` for a local binary or `graft publish --target linux-x64` to deploy.
Both commands place the binary, `.env.example` and this README in `bin/` by default.
When the app starts it prints its App and Swagger URLs. On Windows, startup errors
also appear in a dialog when the executable was opened by double-clicking it.

This project starts with {{.Database.Name}} migration configuration. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and edit APP_PORT and DATABASE_URL. The generated
application reads `.env` beside its executable and also the current working directory;
the latter wins if both exist. `graft dev` and migration commands read the working
directory. Existing environment variables take precedence, including empty values.
`.env.example` itself is never loaded. Keep `.env` out of source control.

Create SQL with `graft make:migration create_machines_table`, edit the Up/Down
sections, then run `graft migrate`. Inspect `graft migrate:status`; undo the most
recent batch with `graft migrate:rollback`, or individual migrations with `--step N`.
Applied migrations must not be edited or removed.

Use `graft new --database postgres` or `graft new --database mysql` in scripts to
skip the selector. SQL files are database-specific; Graft does not translate SQL.
For MySQL 8.0+, DDL can partially commit on error. A dirty migration blocks further
operations. Run `graft migrate:doctor`, then inspect and restore the actual database
before using `graft migrate:repair --plan` and `--confirm`; see docs/MYSQL.md in
the Graft framework. App queries
still use the driver's database/sql API; setting the environment does not rewrite
application code.

Projects use the released Graft Go module, so VS Code, `go test` and `go build`
work normally. `graft dev`, `graft build` and `graft publish` remain convenient
shortcuts. Use `--framework <path>` only to develop against a local checkout.
Commit both `go.mod` and `go.sum`. When upgrading the CLI, the app keeps its pinned
framework version until you explicitly upgrade its dependency.

## Runtime configuration (v0.2+ framework)

New applications default to APP_HOST=127.0.0.1. Set APP_HOST=0.0.0.0 explicitly for
container ingress or run `graft dev --host 0.0.0.0` for network-accessible development.
Swagger is enabled under `graft dev`; compiled apps default to docs off. APP_DOCS
explicitly overrides this. The legacy v0.1.x scaffold retains its older behavior.

APP_MAX_CONCURRENT (64), APP_MAX_BODY_BYTES (1048576), and APP_REQUEST_TIMEOUT (10s)
configure bounded work. These are starter values, not capacity guarantees. Pass
request contexts to downstream calls; a cooperative deadline cannot stop code
that ignores cancellation. Authentication/authorization must be added by the app.
See the framework's docs/PRODUCTION.md and Machines example before deploying.
